## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical end-to-end AWS AI/ML data-extraction workflow: Textract for line items, a lightweight mapping layer, and consistent storage/indexing. Emphasizes event-driven patterns, data correctness, security, and cost awareness at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts

- Textract AnalyzeExpense for invoices
- S3 event triggers and Lambda orchestration
- Lightweight rules-based mapping to cost codes
- DynamoDB upsert pattern for the invoice index
- Data privacy: encryption (SSE-KMS), access logs, CloudTrail
- Reliability: retries, dead-letter queues (DLQ)
- Cost considerations: Textract usage, storage, DynamoDB writes

## Code Example

```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const textract = new AWS.Textract();
const dynamodb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const bucket = event.Records[0].s3.bucket.name;
  const key = decodeURIComponent(event.Records[0].s3.object.key.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
  const obj = await s3.getObject({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key }).promise();

  const resp = await textract.analyzeExpense({ Document: { Bytes: obj.Body } }).promise();
  // Simplified: extract total and line items from resp, map to cost codes
  const invoice = {
    invoiceId: key.split('/').pop().split('.')[0],
    s3Key: key,
    totalAmount: 0,
    currency: 'USD',
    status: 'OPEN'
  };

  // Persist structured JSON to S3 and upsert DynamoDB (pseudo-logic)
  // await s3.putObject({ Bucket: 'invoices-processed', Key: `${invoice.invoiceId}.json`, Body: JSON.stringify(invoice) }).promise();
  // await dynamodb.put({ TableName: 'Invoices', Item: invoice }).promise();
  return;
};
```

